// Regression test for a real hardware/codegen bug found and fixed this
// session: STMT_IF's codegen (codegen.c) used to emit a real SYNC opcode at
// the taken group's landing pad, but warp_stack.sv only pushes once per
// divergence -- two SYNCs (two pops) against one push caused the scheduler
// to prematurely restore active_mask to all-threads before the taken
// group's then-body had executed, silently hijacking every thread's
// instruction stream onto whichever thread core.sv's active_pc mux picked.
// The fix: the landing pad is now a NOP (no sync_en, no pop), so only the
// final, true reconvergence point pops the warp stack.
//
// Every if/ifelse test before this one used blockDim=1 or a uniform
// condition, so real per-thread divergence (some threads taken, some not,
// on the SAME if) had never actually been exercised on hardware. This test
// chains three sequential ifs with a genuinely different taken/not-taken
// split each time -- exactly the pattern the Phase 5 softmax kernel's
// rowmax step needs.
kernel void ifdivergetest() {
    int i = threadIdx;
    int m = mem[i * 4 + 0];
    if (mem[i * 4 + 1] > m) { m = mem[i * 4 + 1]; }
    if (mem[i * 4 + 2] > m) { m = mem[i * 4 + 2]; }
    if (mem[i * 4 + 3] > m) { m = mem[i * 4 + 3]; }
    mem[16 + i] = m;
}
